{"term":"upadła kobieta","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"fallen woman","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":28,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A fallen woman; an archaic phrase for a woman who has lost her sexual virtue and social standing through pre-marital sex, seduction, or prostitution.","english_term":"fallen woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct calque of Victorian English 'fallen woman'; widely used in 19th–20th century Polish literature and Catholic pastoral texts; parallels French 'femme déchue' and Russian 'padshaya zhenshchina'; the 'fall' metaphor implies irreversible moral descent; still understood today but used mainly in archaic or ironic registers."}
{"term":"niesławna","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"ill repute","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":33,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Infamous or notorious (feminine form); used as an adjective or noun for a woman of ill repute, particularly one known for sexual immorality or moral looseness.","english_term":"infamous","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine form of 'niesławny' (inglorious/infamous); in sexual contexts implies public notoriety for sexual misconduct; more literary than spoken; focuses on reputation rather than any specific act; used as a substantivized adjective ('ta niesławna' = that infamous woman)."}
{"term":"dziwka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Whore; the primary and most loaded Polish slur for a sexually promiscuous woman or prostitute, functioning as both a literal reference to sex work and a general misogynistic insult.","english_term":"whore","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Kurwa' (originally also a whore-slur) has largely shifted to a general expletive, leaving 'dziwka' as the more specifically sexual insult; covers both literal prostitution and general promiscuity; context determines which reading applies; among the most severe misogynistic slurs in Polish."}
{"term":"szlaja","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A slut or sexually promiscuous woman; a crude insult for a woman perceived as sexually loose, with connotations of aimless wandering and disreputable character.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymology related to 'szlajać się' (to wander/roam aimlessly); implies the woman drifts from partner to partner without purpose or fidelity; somewhat regional; slightly less extreme than 'dziwka' but still strongly pejorative; also applied to women considered generally low-class or slovenly."}
{"term":"szlajać się","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual promiscuity","concept":"promiscuity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To sleep around; a crude reflexive verb meaning to drift promiscuously from partner to partner, with connotations of aimlessness, moral looseness, and disrepute.","english_term":"to sleep around","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary literal meaning is 'to wander/loiter aimlessly'; the sexual meaning frames promiscuity as purposeless drifting — sexual loyalty is figured as rootedness, promiscuity as homelessness; applied to both sexes but carries far greater stigma when used of women."}
{"term":"puszczać się","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual permissiveness","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To put out; a common colloquial phrase meaning to be sexually permissive or easy, used pejoratively for a woman who has sex readily or with multiple partners.","english_term":"to put out","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'to release or let go of oneself'; the idiom applies the sense of releasing physical restraint to releasing sexual restraint; almost always applied to women; a man who 'puszcza się' faces negligible stigma by comparison; nominal derivative 'puszczalska' is its pejorative agent noun."}
{"term":"puszczalska","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":75,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A woman who puts out easily; a pejorative noun for a sexually permissive woman derived from 'puszczać się,' labeling her as sexually available and of low moral standards.","english_term":"easy woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Suffix '-alska' creates a feminine agent noun denoting habitual action; more specifically targets sexual availability than 'dziwka,' less overtly linked to prostitution; widely recognized across all age groups; commonly used among teenagers and young adults."}
{"term":"szmatka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'small rag or cloth'; a crude insult for a woman of sexual looseness or low moral character, dehumanizing her by comparing her to a used, disposable cleaning rag.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Object metaphor implies the woman has been used by multiple men and is now soiled and discarded; diminutive '-ka' softens the sound slightly without reducing the sting; also applied to women simply regarded as low-class or slovenly regardless of sexual behavior."}
{"term":"flejtucha","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A slovenly and sexually immoral woman; an older insult combining connotations of physical untidiness and sexual looseness, suggesting a woman who is neither clean in appearance nor morally restrained.","english_term":"slattern","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The fusion of physical slovenliness with sexual looseness distinguishes 'flejtucha' from pure slut-shaming vocabulary; closest English equivalent is archaic 'slattern'; declining in use among younger speakers; more rural and regional in distribution."}
{"term":"pryska","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A crude slang term for a sexually promiscuous woman; a coarse insult labeling a woman as sexually available and of low moral character.","english_term":"slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the less common Polish slut-shame terms; etymology uncertain, possibly related to 'pryskać' (to spray/squirt) with sexual innuendo; recognition and usage vary significantly by region and generation; more common in colloquial speech than in media or print."}
